export const JT_DOCS = `== Joshua Tree ==
Joshua Tree is an operating system: A whole computer, built from scratch: its own windows, dock, fonts, sound, internet and 31 apps, from the first chip instruction to the last pixel. No libc, no dependencies. It also runs live in a browser tab at joshuatree.heyitsmejosh.com, the same kernel, not a recording. Joshua Trommel is building it, and why: Every computer I have ever used was someone else's decisions, stacked a thousand deep, none of which I could see. I wanted one machine where I understand the whole thing. Not "trust the abstraction." Actually know why that bit is set in that page table. The longer goal is a computer I built end to end: this software, on hardware I can hold, talking to a model with as little cloud in the loop as possible. This is the software half. The ideas behind each call are in docs/SOUL.md and docs/VISION.md. It is licensed Apache License 2.0, copyright 2026 Joshua Trommel, free and open source, code on GitHub at github.com/nulljosh/joshuatree.

== Version and booting it ==
The current version of Joshua Tree is 2.47.0. Versions follow semver (major.minor.patch): minor for a new capability, patch for a fix. Try it live in a browser at joshuatree.heyitsmejosh.com, no install. To run it as the real OS on a PC, write the ISO from GitHub Releases to a USB stick and boot from it. It also runs on a real Raspberry Pi 4 from an SD card (an ARM64 build): the desktop at 1080p, a USB keyboard, Wi-Fi, sound, its own SD card for files, and Samantha. Joshua Tree is written from nothing, for 32-bit Intel PCs (i386) and the Pi: its own bootloader, memory manager, filesystem, network stack, window system and typeface renderer, no Linux or any other OS underneath it. It is written in C, with a little assembly.

== Apps and the dock ==
Every app lives in the Apps folder; the dock pins a handful of them: the Apps folder, Burrow, Mail, Calendar, Notes, Reminders, Terminal, Samantha, Weather, Stocks, Trash.
Mail: Mail reads and sends email.
Calendar: Calendar shows the month and the events on each day.
Notes: Folders and many notes under NOTES/; an old NOTES.TXT moves in as the first note.
Reminders: Reminders is a to-do list you can tick off.
Terminal: Terminal is the same shell the machine boots into, just running in a window.
Samantha: Samantha is the assistant you are talking to: ask a question, or ask her to open an app.
Weather: Weather pulls a live forecast from Open-Meteo by IP geolocation, shown in the menu bar and in its own app.
Curbfind: Craigslist deals near you, ranked by score, live through SYS_HTTP_GET with Vancouver samples when offline.
Keyrate: Keyrate is a real native typing test: a word list, a live input loop, your words-per-minute.
Bookrank: Ranked non-fiction: a list on the left, the selected book's title, author, rating, review count and summary on the right.
Quotes: Name the film from the line.
Tonchi: Learn anything: pick a live course, then a four-choice drill with a score.
Toroid: Conway's Life on a torus.
Hikko: The idea forum: the top ideas from the real forum, vote on them for the session only.
Fieldbook: Every field of science and math, explained plainly.
Contacts: Contacts keeps names, numbers and emails.
Calculator: Recursive-descent parser over + - / ().
Stocks: Eight fixed symbols with live quotes and charts from the Worker at /api/stocks; the UI is user/stocks.c.
Search: Search finds files as you type.
Epiphany: Watchlist, portfolio, simulator and a Bloomberg-style command bar (/, then AAPL GP or AAPL DES), live prices through SYS_HTTP_GET.
Activity: Activity Monitor over the real scheduler and memory counters.
Clock: Current time from the RTC, a countdown timer you can start and pause, and an alarm.
Music: Music plays the songs you own, MP3 or WAV, from Files.
Movies: Plays motion-JPEG AVI clips from Files with sound, pause, seek and fullscreen.
Hamurapi: Hamurapi is the 1968 game Hamurabi, rebuilt with a living city.
Windgate: Guided breathing: a circle grows on the in-breath, holds, shrinks on the out-breath.
Panes: Terminal tabs and split panes, cmux-style: a tab rail, a shell per pane, an activity dot on a tab that printed in the background.
Claude: Ask Claude Code a question from inside Joshua Tree.
Mines: Minesweeper, the 9x9 beginner board.

== Accounts and Lock Screen ==
Settings has an Add user step: an account's password is salted and hashed, never stored in the clear. Once an account exists, booting shows a real login screen; a wrong password is rejected with a short delay. The tree-logo Apple menu in the top corner has Lock Screen: with an account, it re-locks the machine the same way a reboot's login screen does; with no account yet, it just shows a brief message. The same menu also has About, Files, Notes, Settings, Restart and Shut Down.

== What it does not do ==
On a PC it uses wired networking; on the Raspberry Pi it joins Wi-Fi itself, and a Bluetooth mouse is being added. It speaks HTTPS with its own TLS. Samantha, the assistant inside it, answers from Turing, Joshua's own model, through a small relay on his Mac, with no cloud API key. Not done yet: many windows at once on the Pi, and most desktop apps still need their own Pi port.`;
